NNAAMMEE
       pamenlarge - Enlarge a Netpbm image N times


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammeennllaarrggee  [--ssccaallee==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r] [--xxssccaallee==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r] [--yyssccaallee==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r] [_f_i_l_e_‐
       _n_a_m_e]

       ppaammeennllaarrggee _N [_p_n_m_f_i_l_e]



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammeennllaarrggee reads a Netpbm image  as  input,  replicates  its  pixels  _N
       times,  and  produces a Netpbm image as output.  The output is the same
       type of image as the input.

       If you enlarge by a factor of 3 or more, you should probably add a  ppnn‐‐
       mmssmmooootthh step; otherwise, you can see the original pixels in the result‐
       ing image.

       For PBM images, ppaammeennllaarrggee uses special fast algorithms for scale  fac‐
       tors  up  to  10.   For larger factors, it uses a simple but slow algo‐
       rithm.  As a result, you can often get a significantly faster scale  by
       running  ppaammeennllaarrggee  multiple  times.   For example, enlarging by 3 and
       then by 5 is faster than enlarging once by 15.  And because  the  algo‐
       rithms  are different for the different scale factors, some faster than
       others, the order matters too.  For example, the following examples all
       produce  the  same  output -- an image 15 times bigger on edge than the
       input -- but at different speeds, each being faster than  the  one  be‐
       fore.

            $ pamenlarge -scale=15 test.pbm
            $ pamenlarge -scale=5 test.pbm | pamenlarge -scale=3
            $ pamenlarge -scale=3 test.pbm | pamenlarge -scale=5


       The  special fast cases for factors up to 10 have existed since Release
       10.50 (March 2010).  The special cases for 1, 2, 3, and 5  go  back  to
       Release  10.41  (December  2007).   Before  10.41, there are no special
       scale factors and PBM enlargement is significantly  slower  than  today
       for all scale factors.

       ppaammeennllaarrggee  can  enlarge  only by integer factors.  The slower but more
       general ppaammssccaallee can enlarge or reduce by arbitrary factors.   ppaammssccaallee
       allows  you to enlarge by resampling, which gives you smoother enlarge‐
       ments.  But it is much slower.

       ppaammssttrreettcchh is another enlarging program that enlarges by  integer  fac‐
       tors.   It  does  a simple kind of resampling that gives you a smoothed
       enlargement with less computational cost.

       ppbbmmrreedduuccee can reduce by integer factors, but only for PBM images.


AARRGGUUMMEENNTTSS
       As with most Netpbm programs, you can give the input file  name  as  an
       argument  or  omit  that  argument and have it come from Standard Input
       (and you can specify '-' for the argument to specify Standard Input ex‐
       plicitly).

       You can also specify the scale factor as an argument, for backward com‐
       patibility, but the preferred way to do that is with a  --ssccaallee  option,
       because  it  is easier to remember and read that way.  The scale factor
       argument goes before the file name argument.





OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppaammeennllaarrggee recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --ssccaallee==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r

       --xxssccaallee==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r

       --yyssccaallee==_i_n_t_e_g_e_r
              These specify the scale factor.  --xxssccaallee specifies the  horizon‐
              tal  scale  factor;  --yyssccaallee specifies the vertical scale factor
              and --ssccaallee specifies both.

              If you specify --xxssccaallee but  not  --yyssccaallee,  ppaammeennllaarrggee  does  not
              scale  vertically  (i.e.  the  vertical scale factor is 1).  The
              converse applies if you specify --yyssccaallee and not --xxssccaallee.

              You cannot specify --ssccaallee and also --xxssccaallee or yyssccaallee.

              You must specify at least one of these options, unless  you  use
              the  deprecaated method of specifying the scale factor via argu‐
              ment.

              These options were all new in Netpbm 10.86 (March 2019).  Before
              that, use the scale argument.





HHIISSTTOORRYY
       ppaammeennllaarrggee was new in Netpbm 10.25 (October 2004).  It is designed as a
       replacement for ppnnmmeennllaarrggee by Jef Poskanzer, which was  in  Pbmplus  as
       far  back as 1989.  The major difference is that ppaammeennllaarrggee can enlarge
       PAM format images in addition to PNM.



